\begin_layout Subsubsection
Graphene and van der Waals' heterostructures
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In 1947 P R Wallace attempted to explain the electronic properties of graphite
 by considering the electronic structure of the individual graphitic basal
 planes, finding each 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

to be a semi-conductor with zero activation energy
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wallace1947theband"

\end_inset

 This work captured the essence of the unusual properties a single graphitic
 sheet would have, though they proved to be far stranger.
 In 2004 Nobel laureates Prof Andre Geim and Prof Konstantin Novoselov were
 the first to successfully isolate and characterise free standing graphene
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "novoselov2004electric"

\end_inset

, although it should be acknowledged that monolayer graphite was known prior
 to this
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "geim2007therise"

\end_inset

.
 They did this using the 'Scotch tape' method, where micromechanical cleavage
 is achieved using sticky tape to pull apart thick highly oriented pyrolytic
 graphite until it is atomically thin.
 The first electronic transport measurements not only showed that graphene's
 band structure has 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Dirac cones
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 at the K-points of the Brillouin zone, and that charge carriers close to
 the centre of the cone behave as massless Dirac fermions, but that the
 anomalous quantum Hall effect and Berry's phase could be experimentally
 measured in high quality graphene flakes.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "novoselov2005twodimensional,zhang2005experimental"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These phenomena all arise from two fundamental features of graphene's electronic
 structure.
 Firstly, charge carriers in graphene are chiral: Graphene has two sublattices
 which both contribute to the wavefunction of charge carriers, effectively
 burdoning them with an additional quantum number, named psuedospin.
 Psuedospin sets charge carriers in graphene apart from other two dimensional
 electron gases as this additional property suppresses the backscattering
 of carriers.
 Broadly, this means that exotic quantum mechanical phenomena are more likely
 to be observed in graphene than in more traditional condensed matter systems
 where scattering can dominate measurements.
 A surprising manifestation of this effect is the Klein paradox, where carriers
 will always propogate through a sharp barrier if the angle of incidence
 is 0° to the barrier normal.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "katsnelson2006chiraltunnelling,young2009quantum,wilmart2014akleintunneling"

\end_inset

 Graphene also has a unique electronic bandstructure: a gapless, linear
 energy spectrum for electrons and holes.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "geim2009graphene,castroneto2009theelectronic"

\end_inset

 Charge carriers in normal semiconductors can be described by a relatively
 straightforward Schrödinger equation, where the energy and effective mass
 varies parabolically.
 In graphene, the Schrödinger equation is not applicable to the linear energy
 spectrum.
 Instead the charge carriers must be described by a 2D relativistic Dirac
 equation as they are massless about the linear region; thus massless Dirac
 fermions.
 This is the unique case of monolayer graphene.
 In bilayer graphene an interesting combination of Dirac and Schrödinger
 equations results in massive Dirac fermions with four possible psuedospin
 states.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since then graphene has been a hot topic of research with the original Science
 paper recieving over 21,000 citations, placing it in the top 100 most cited
 papers.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "vannoorden2014thetop"

\end_inset

 Monolayer graphene exhibited carrier mobilities of up to 200,000 cm
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2 
\end_layout

\end_inset

V
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
-1 
\end_layout

\end_inset

s
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
-1
\end_layout

\end_inset

at low temperature in the highest quality devices 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "chen2008intrinsic,bolotin2008ultrahigh"

\end_inset

, but, more interestingly, mobilities upwards of 15,000 cm
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2 
\end_layout

\end_inset

V
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
-1
\end_layout

\end_inset

 s
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
-1
\end_layout

\end_inset

at room temperature
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "geim2007therise"

\end_inset

.
 It was not long before it was realised that the thin silicon dioxide substrate,
 which was relied upon to reveal single layer graphene under the optical
 microscope
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "blake2007makinggraphene"

\end_inset

 and insulate graphene from the silicon back gate
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "novoselov2005twodimensional,zhang2005experimental"

\end_inset

, was breaking up the 2D electron gas in graphene through surface roughness,
 small areas of inhomogenous surface charging and impurities.
 The device performance was significantly improved by inserting a thin layer
 of insulating hexagonal boron nitride (hBN) between graphene and the substrate.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "dean2010boronnitride"

\end_inset

This heralded a step change in graphene device quality and was one of the
 first instances of a van der Waals' heterostructure.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "geim2013vander"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
White graphene: the perfect dielectric
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
hBN is an insulating isomorph of graphene, sometimes dubbed 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

white graphene
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, with strong in-plane ionic bonds in place of graphene's covalent ones.
 Bulk hBN glues inert sheets of hexagonal boron and nitrogen lattices together
 with van der Waals' bonds, similar to graphite.
 When thin sheets of graphene are placed on top of hBN and annealed, surface
 contaminants pool together in bubbles in what has become known as the self-clea
ning phenomenon.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "lui2009ultraflat,haigh2012crosssectional,georgiou2013vertical"

\end_inset

 Self-cleaning arises from the van der Waals' attraction between hBN and
 graphene
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "khestanova2016graphene"

\end_inset

 and results in large areas of atomically clean interfaces, proving vital
 for high quality measurements.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "dean2010boronnitride"

\end_inset

 These humble properties have allowed some of the most exciting physical
 phenomena to be realised, including negative differential conductance
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "britnell2013resonant"

\end_inset

, Hofstadter’s butterfly in bilayer graphene 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "dean2013hofstadters"

\end_inset

 and Klein tunneling of the chiral Dirac fermions.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "katsnelson2006chiraltunnelling,young2009quantum,wilmart2014akleintunneling"

\end_inset


\end_layout

