\begin_layout Subsubsection
Device fabrication
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Discoveries such as these have largely only been possible due to progress
 in 2D crystal transfer techniques.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "kang2012graphene"

\end_inset

 Broadly, there are two methods used to transfer one mechanically exfoliated
 2D crystal on top of another: dry transfer and wet transfer, although other
 techniques are possible for different synthesis routes.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "chen2016progress"

\end_inset

 Wet transfer uses a sacrificial water soluble polymer layer to transfer
 2D flakes.
 The sacraficial layer is spin coated onto a silicon substrate and then
 further coated with polymethyl-meth-acrylate (PMMA).
 Graphite is mechanically exfoliated onto this polymer stack and thin flakes
 identified by optical microscopy
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "blake2007makinggraphene"

\end_inset

 and Raman spectroscopy
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ferrari2006ramanspectrum"

\end_inset

.
 To isolate a suitable flake, the stack is floated on the surface of a water
 bath until the water soluble polyer dissolved, allowing the silicon substrate
 to sink.
 A glass slide with a cut hole in the middle is then manouvered with a micromani
pulator over the flake on the floating PMMA to remove them from the bath,
 all observed under an optical microscope.
 The PMMA is flipped over so that the flake-side is facing the bench.
 The flake is aligned above a target substrate flake (e.g.
 hBN) and the two brought into contact to make a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

stack
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 The PMMA is then dissolved with acetone and washed away.
 The stack is annealled to > 110 °C to drive off solvents and contamination.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Dry transfer is a variation on the wet transfer technique which attempts
 to minimise contamination between flakes and greatly speed up the transfer
 process.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "withers2015lightemitting,wang2013onedimensional,kretinin2014electronic"

\end_inset

 The wet transfer procedure is followed to the point where a flake is isolated
 on a PMMA membrane above a smaller target flake.
 Once the two flakes are in contact, instead of dissolving the PMMA, the
 PMMA is lifted from the substrate bringing the two flakes with it.
 This step is then repeated for many flakes to build up a bespoke complex
 stack of different 2D materials.
 On the last transfer step, the PMMA is dissolved with acetone and the stack
 annealled.
 Through this method the majority of interfaces between flakes will be isolated
 from PMMA and solvents.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Stacks of 2D crystals are made into devices by adding metallic contacts
 to the surface
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "britnell2013resonant,gorbachev2007weaklocalization,gorbachev2014detecting"

\end_inset

 or edges of graphene sheets
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang2013onedimensional,kretinin2014electronic"

\end_inset

.
 More recently, in the latest generation of devices, metal contacts are
 placed on the surface of an encapsulating hBN layer so that electrons can
 be injected by tunnelling into buried conductive layers or used as a gate
 to probe the electronic properties of a device.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "britnell2012electron,britnell2012fieldeffect,britnell2013stronglightmatter,georgiou2013vertical,withers2014heterostructures,cao2015quality"

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Transition metal dichalcogenides
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The transition metal dichalcogenides (TMDCs) are metallic or semiconducting
 crystals of formula MX
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

, where M is a transition metal (e.g.
 molybdenum, tungsten, niobium, etc.) and X is a chalcogen (sulfur, selenium
 or tellerium).
 Similarly to graphene and hBN, a TMDC has hexagonal lattice sheets with
 strong in-plane bonding.
 Each sheet is stacked on top of another and held by van der Waals' forces.
 However, unlike graphene and hBN, they are not one atomic layer but three,
 with a sheet of transition metal atoms encapsulated by two sheets of chalcogen
 atoms.
 The most popular TMDCs are currently MoS
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

, WS
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

, MoSe
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

and WSe
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

 due to them having an indirect semiconducting band gap in bulk crystals
 and a direct gap in monolayer crystals.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "mak2010atomically,splendiani2010emerging"

\end_inset

 The direct band gaps of the popular TMDCs range from ~1.5 - 2.1 eV
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang2012electronics"

\end_inset

, which correspond to near infrared - yellow photon energies in the visible
 spectrum.
 Furthermore, these TMDC monolayers are 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

multivalley semiconductors
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, meaning they have favourable and distinct localised states which a charge
 carrier wavefunction can occupy.
 These states appear as valleys at the K points of the Brillouin zone (K
 and K- in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Liu_introTMDCstruc"

\end_inset

), with differences between valleys arising from a lack of inversion symmetry
 in the crystal structure of monolayer TMDCs.
 A carrier can be confined to one of these valleys and so gains an extra
 constraint: a 
\emph on
k
\emph default
-valley index (also known as valley degree of freedom or valley pseudospin).
 In addition to this, semiconducting TMDCs show strong spin-orbit coupling
 which arises from the valence electron states originating from d-orbitals.
 This results in spin up and spin down carriers having different energies
 as spin degeneracy is removed from the conduction and valence bands.
\end_layout

